      "headline": "Mexico's annual inflation falls to 3.12 percent in July, its lowest rate since 2020",
      "context": "The headline rate is close to the Mexican central bank's target, but underlying inflation remained more persistent.",
      "background": "Headline inflation includes food and energy prices that can move sharply. Core inflation removes the most volatile items and is usually a better guide to persistent price pressure.",
      "view": "The gap matters because volatile food and energy prices can pull the headline down before persistent prices improve. The current reading therefore says less about lasting inflation than the 3.12 percent figure suggests.",
      "prediction": "The September 9 release will show whether the decline has reached core prices. If core inflation falls, the July improvement is broader. If it rises, the 3.12 percent headline will have overstated the progress.",
